+/*
+ * Completion timeout for a single IPU op, and the consecutive-wedge fuse
+ * for the reset+retry mitigation in ipu_start().
+ *
+ * The T20-family IPU occasionally wedges mid-frame into an undefined
+ * internal state (MSTATUS=3 in IPU_STATUS[5:4]; hardware documents only
+ * 0/1/2) roughly once per ~500k ops.  The engine clock stays alive and
+ * counting, the IRQ stays enabled, but OUT_END never asserts - a silicon
+ * datapath hang, not a software bug.  There is no software cure; the
+ * existing IPU_STOP/IPU_RESET trigger sequence reliably returns the
+ * engine to a working state, so all we can do is detect fast and recover
+ * fast instead of parking both video channels behind ipu->mutex for the
+ * old 2000 ms wait.
+ *
+ * Timeout budget, from live T20 register evidence (Wyze Cam v2, JXF23):
+ * IPU_CLK_NUM_ONE_FRA counts IPU clocks per frame.  Healthy ops measure
+ * ~0x6E00 (~28k clocks) for small ops and ~0x26500 (~157k clocks) for a
+ * full 1920x1088 composite.  During a wedged 2000 ms wait the same
+ * counter reads 0x17d3df38 (400.1M clocks), which both proves the clock
+ * ran the whole time and directly measures the IPU clock at ~200 MHz.
+ * Worst healthy op is therefore ~0.8 ms; 100 ms is >120x headroom, so a
+ * timeout is a wedge, not a slow frame.
+ */
+#define IPU_DONE_TIMEOUT_MS	100
+
+/*
+ * If this many consecutive ops time out (a transient wedge clears after
+ * one reset; a genuinely dead engine does not), stop paying for the
+ * in-call retry and fail each op after a single timeout instead.  Any
+ * successful op resets the streak.  The retry is already bounded to one
+ * per call, so this fuse only trims worst-case latency on broken
+ * hardware; 16 ops (~a third of a second of stalled ops at 50 ops/s) is
+ * ample evidence the engine is not coming back on its own.
+ */
+#define IPU_WEDGE_STREAK_FUSE	16
